<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      The pre-Compose window background, light. Must track SwipeyLightColors.canvas in
      ui/design/Tokens.kt — the two are the same surface, a few milliseconds apart.
    -->
    <color name="swipey_window">#FBFBFD</color>

    <!--
      The ground behind the launcher icon's mark. The app's own canvas, exactly.

      This reverses an earlier decision and the reason it was made is worth keeping, because
      it is a real risk: a dark tile among the light ones a launcher is full of can recede
      into the wallpaper instead of reading, and an earlier cut of this icon did exactly
      that. What is different now is where the silhouette lives. That version put a mid-tone
      mark on the dark ground, so the *tile* had to carry the shape and it had nothing to
      carry it against. This one puts a near-white card on it at about 18:1 — the ink is the
      brightest thing in the tile, so the mark reads even where its edge does not, and the
      icon is a lit card on black rather than a black square with something in it.

      It must NEVER gain a values-night counterpart. A launcher icon is a fixed piece of
      artwork: it keeps the same ground whether the phone is in light or dark mode, the same
      way every other icon on the home screen does. Give it a night override and the app's
      identity changes shape at sunset.
    -->
    <color name="swipey_icon_ground">#0E0F11</color>
</resources>
